WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:02.823
- [Announcer] Flight to New York, JFK.

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:06.400
- [Narrator] Giulio Castagnara
is flying to New York

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:08.220
and before he can board his flight,

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:10.910
he has to prove that he's COVID free.

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:12.065
- [Giulio] You download
your app on your phone

00:00:12.065 --> 00:00:13.780
and it has all your information.

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:15.480
- [Narrator] This is AOKpass,

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:17.560
a digital health passport that verifies

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:19.010
whether someone has tested negative

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:21.860
for the coronavirus or gotten inoculated.

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:24.140
It's also known as a vaccine passport

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:25.480
and meant to be easier to use

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:28.250
and more reliable than
paper immunization cards.

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:30.330
- Medical results often
are not even printed

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:33.640
in a way that it's easy to interpret.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:36.970
Our system can verify health records.

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:37.803
- [Narrator] Since the pandemic

00:00:37.803 --> 00:00:39.750
has devastated the travel industry,

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:41.970
airports in Rome, Paris and Singapore,

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:44.480
as well as airlines
like JetBlue and United

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:46.910
are experimenting with similar apps.

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:49.360
They will compete for a
slice of the global market

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:52.200
for travel health services
and certifications,

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:56.400
which AOKpass estimates is
worth nearly $20 billion.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.530
Travel industry experts say this type

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:00.140
of digital health certificate

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:03.100
could be a game changer,
not just for tourism

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:05.320
but live events and schools.

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:07.903
So here's how digital
health passports work.

00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:12.050
Travelers who weren't able
to take a COVID-19 test

00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:14.400
before departure at this airport in Rome

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.720
can get swabbed at a testing
site set up by the airport.

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:19.090
While waiting for the result,

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:21.630
passengers can download the AOKpass app

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:23.390
and input their personal information

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:25.920
like their birthday and email address.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:28.490
Within 30 minutes, the
result is available.

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:29.970
If the traveler tests negative,

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:33.390
the app creates a unique QR
code that serves as proof.

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:36.154
- The doctor's scanning like the QR code

00:01:36.154 --> 00:01:39.197
that says that you're negative
and you're good to go.

00:01:39.197 --> 00:01:41.231
(announcement dinging)

00:01:41.231 --> 00:01:42.140
- [Narrator] At the airport in Rome,

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:44.400
it has to be scanned at
check in and boarding.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.430
- I think this is the
only way really right now

00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:48.400
to travel like safely

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:51.100
without risking contracting COVID.

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:53.070
- [Narrator] Some but not
all airlines and governments

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:54.670
require proof of a negative test

00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:56.090
before a passenger is allowed

00:01:56.090 --> 00:01:58.720
to board a flight or enter a country.

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:00.780
While testing and
vaccinations reduce the risk

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.916
of spreading the virus,
they don't eliminate it.

00:02:03.916 --> 00:02:06.050
In the setting of COVID-19,

00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:09.440
health information is beginning to be used

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:13.360
in a transactional sense,
much more than before.

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:14.700
- [Narrator] Chester Drum is a doctor

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:17.570
at the National University
Hospital in Singapore

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:19.710
and co-founded AOKpass.

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:21.310
He expects that in order for people

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:23.350
to return to pre-pandemic life,

00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:26.130
they'll have to show proof
of their health status.

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:28.270
- Let me show you part
of my medical record

00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:29.640
to get on an airplane.

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:34.440
Let me show you that I've
had a vaccination procedure

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:37.400
in order to go to an event, for example.

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:39.500
- [Narrator] This has
also meant forgeries.

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:42.380
For instance, last November,
seven people in Paris

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:43.630
were arrested and charged

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:46.070
with selling fake COVID-19 test results

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:49.140
that cost as much as $360.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:51.010
Drum says the app fights counterfeiting

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:52.760
using blockchain technology

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:55.330
so that the test result and
vaccination certificates

00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:58.050
are unique and linked
to only one individual.

00:02:58.050 --> 00:02:59.840
- The remarkable thing about blockchain

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:02.180
is once it's entered, you can't chain it.

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:03.520
- [Narrator] While blockchain-backed apps

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:06.070
may prevent fakes, there
could be data leaks.

00:03:06.070 --> 00:03:09.530
- They pose a variety
of essential questions

00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:13.600
for the production of data
privacy and human rights.

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:15.190
- [Narrator] Ana Beduschi is a researcher

00:03:15.190 --> 00:03:16.980
who studies the ethical implication

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:18.500
of digital health data.

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:20.950
- Policymakers should require

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:22.870
digital health password providers

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:25.850
to adopt appropriate technical

00:03:25.850 --> 00:03:28.420
and organizational measures to address

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:32.440
potential data privacy invasive
situations practically.

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:33.470
- [Narrator] Public health researchers

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:35.180
have found that some people in India

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:37.110
and Indonesia who tested positive

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:38.920
and had their results disclosed

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:41.770
were verbally attacked
or socially excluded.

00:03:41.770 --> 00:03:44.490
In the US, the Centers for
Disease Control and Prevention

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:47.430
recommend that community leaders
and public health officials

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:49.720
maintained the confidentiality
of test results

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:51.323
to avoid social stigma.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:55.620
AOKpass says it encrypts all
personal health information

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:57.990
and tries to keep to a
minimum the data it collects

00:03:57.990 --> 00:03:59.180
on its users.

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:01.740
The company also said it
does not monetize data

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:04.140
but has signed contracts
with airports and airlines

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:06.250
to develop and test the system.

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:08.040
After nearly 10 months of testing,

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:11.180
AOKpass is scheduled to
launch in all major Italian

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:13.980
and French airports by
the end of February.

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:16.420
It will have to compete with
other companies developing

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:17.730
a similar tool.

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:20.610
United and JetBlue are preparing
to use an app developed

00:04:20.610 --> 00:04:23.860
by the World Economic Forum
called the CommonPass,

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:25.230
while IBM is creating

00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:28.210
a digital health pass using blockchain.

00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:30.210
Drum says figuring out the technical parts

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:33.180
of the digital health
passport were relatively easy,

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:35.050
compared with making sure
the app would be used

00:04:35.050 --> 00:04:37.780
along each step of the travel process.

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:41.620
- What's hard is the coordinated process,

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:44.530
starting with a laboratory
in a different country,

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:46.810
creating a foreign medical record,

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:48.130
boarding an airline,

00:04:48.130 --> 00:04:50.660
another highly regulated industry

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:53.050
and then crossing an immigration gantry,

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:55.140
which itself is regulated

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:58.840
by the rules and regulations
of a country different

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:01.430
than where you got your health test.

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:03.070
- When Castagnara arrived in New York

00:05:03.070 --> 00:05:05.260
for a layover on his way to Dallas,

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:07.610
he says airline staff
didn't ask to see proof

00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:09.140
of his health status.

00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:11.810
The Rome Airport Authority said
they're educating travelers

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:14.890
about the app and plan
to reach out to airlines.

00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:16.820
Despite the patchy use of the app,

00:05:16.820 --> 00:05:19.100
Castagnara says the
digital health passport

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:20.340
is a game changer.

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:21.910
- [Giulio] Last time I
traveled to the States

00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:26.160
was March of 2020, right
before the pandemic hit,

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:28.523
so I mean, it's nice to travel again.

